|-----------|------|------------------------|------------|
| CodeGenerator | CodeGenerator.cs | 0.85 | High |
| UrlSafety | UrlSafety.cs | 0.90 | High |
| LinkRepository | LinkRepository.cs | 0.88 | High |
| LinkService | LinkService.cs | 0.88 | High |
| Program.cs | Program.cs | 0.30 | Low (only liveness endpoint tested; handlers not unit-tested) |
| **Overall** | **all .cs files** | **0.82** | **Medium** |

## Verdict

The test suite provides strong unit-level coverage of the core domain logic: URL validation, code generation, persistence, and service orchestration. It catches the acceptance criteria and common failure modes (invalid input, conflict, expiry, missing resource).

The main gaps are:
1. Integration/HTTP-level tests (endpoint routing, JSON handling, status codes) — suitable for a separate integration test suite.
2. Multi-process concurrency — requires real processes, not unit-testable in isolation.
3. Persistence to disk and schema initialization — requires temporary file fixtures.

These gaps are acceptable for a unit test suite. A complete test strategy would add integration tests to cover them. For the scope of unit testing alone, this suite is fit for purpose.
